-- STATUS: APPLIED 2026-08-04 against league_production
--
-- Restore the last 40 player_gamelogs rows the 8f4292e08 repair deleted that a
-- current-evidence re-adjudication still says are real. This closes the
-- gamelog half of that repair: after it, every one of the 1,560 deleted rows
-- is either back or has a stated reason to stay deleted.
--
-- All 40 belong to `CHRI-SMIT-007265` and span 2014-2018.
--
-- WHY THESE 40. `player_could_have_played` was re-run 2026-08-04 over every
-- still-missing (pid, esbid) pair in the backup against the CURRENT `player`
-- rows -- three commits (38dfc4c32, 8911c494c, b15fc25a6) plus a sibling
-- session's duplicate-person collapse (7eda49a6c) have moved those rows since
-- the backup was taken, so the pre-repair snapshot no longer describes them.
-- 1,272 pairs are absent under their backup pid; 40 pass the predicate and
-- 1,232 are rejected.
--
-- `CHRI-SMIT-007265` is the "biography overwritten" case in
-- text/league/data-quality-and-validation.md: `player_changelog` shows sleeper
-- rewriting nfl_draft_year 2014 -> 2023 in one second, so a 2014 Arkansas DE's
-- row took a 2023 Notre Dame OL's biography. b15fc25a6 put the 2014 draft year
-- back, and with draft_round 5 the predicate now clears every 2014-2018 season.
-- The independent gsis serial cohort agrees and is not derived from either
-- field: 00-0031264 sits in a 63-player block whose nfl_draft_year is 2014
-- unanimously.
--
-- WHY NOT 41. An earlier measurement counted 41, the extra being
-- CORE-TAYL-015520 at esbid 2014080300. That esbid has no `nfl_games` row, so
-- a measurement that reads the season by joining `nfl_games` hands the
-- predicate a null `season_year`, and `player_could_have_played` returns true
-- on a null season by design -- it passed vacuously rather than on evidence.
-- Read against the backup's own season_year of 2014 it is rejected like its
-- sibling row (entry year 2021, undrafted, well outside the two-season grace).
-- Both CORE-TAYL-015520 rows stay deleted.
--
-- 162 OF THE REJECTED ROWS ARE NOT ACTUALLY LOST, they were rekeyed to the
-- right human. 38dfc4c32 restored them under the conflated pid, and the
-- duplicate-person collapse then moved them to the father's own `player` row:
-- all 123 BENN-SAPP-027306 rows now sit on BENJ-SAPP-001764 (born 1981, 148
-- gamelogs spanning 2004-2011) and all 39 SEAN-RYAN-027249 rows on
-- SEAN-RYAN-001783 (born 1980, 78 gamelogs spanning 2004-2010). Restoring them
-- under the backup pid would duplicate them onto the son.
--
-- WHY A BACKUP REPLAY RATHER THAN A REGENERATION -- unchanged from
-- db/adhoc/2026-08-04-restore-conflated-player-gamelogs.sql: roster-only rows
-- carry no play stat for a generator to rebuild from, the snap columns belong
-- to generate-player-snaps.mjs rather than to the gamelog generator, and the
-- generator resolves a retired player's team through `player.current_nfl_team`,
-- which reads 'INA'.
--
-- Source: scratch/repair-name-match-misattribution/gamelogs-residue-before.json
-- (1,560 rows, all 106 columns, captured by the repair before its delete).
--
-- `source` is restored as NULL rather than as the 'nfl-pro-gameday-roster'
-- value the backup carries -- that value was the column DEFAULT, not a writer
-- stamp, so it never named these rows' writer.
--
-- Plain INSERT rather than the precedent's upsert: no CHRI-SMIT-007265 gamelog
-- exists at any of the 40 esbids and no Chris Smith pid holds one either, so a
-- conflict here would mean a concurrent writer got there first and should
-- abort the transaction rather than silently overwrite.
--
-- Expected: 40 rows affected.
--
-- Revert: DELETE FROM player_gamelogs WHERE pid = 'CHRI-SMIT-007265'
--   AND esbid IN (2014090707, 2014102603, 2014110903, 2014113004, 2014121401, 2014122803, 2015092706, 2015100404, 2015101104, 2015101807, 2015102500, 2015110805, 2015111506, 2015111900, 2015120604, 2015122003, 2015122700, 2016010304, 2016091103, 2016091812, 2016092504, 2016101601, 2016102303, 2016110603, 2016111302, 2016112003, 2016112702, 2016121105, 2016121804, 2016122405, 2017010103, 2017100800, 2017102208, 2017123101, 2018101402, 2018102109, 2018102807, 2018110403, 2018112503, 2018123000);

